          %p
            The calculations work out cleanest if we assume if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is algebraically closed. For in that case, we can take advantage of partial fraction decompositions of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mi rev
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi k
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi k
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
